Michal Brzozowski wrote:

 > I'm creating a separate thread, mostly to cut off
 > from the ongoing flamewar in the one started by JG
 > Miller.  I agree that anniversaries are a good
 > time for reflection so let's try to evaluate the
 > current situation of NetHack and its community and
 > what can be done to make things better (as opposed
 > to concentrating on what's wrong with the
 > DevTeam).

If we can ever get past the "in what language" issue

0) no really good language choice puts itself
    forward

    a) C lacks most features of modern programming
       languages, may not be much taught to new
       generations of programmers compared to OOPLs.

    b) C++ is a retrofitted pastiche botch,

    c) Python is spit ugly,

    d) D who?,

    e) Java's owner seems to be going under,

    f) programmers of better languages (OCaml,
       Haskel, Euclid) are hard to find,

    g) "my favorite/only programming language should
        be the choice" is already arising as a plague
        to the project, before there _is_ a project

    h) whatever is chosen needs to be a language that
       is available as freeware, so that any
       interested end user of NetHack-not-in-C will
       be able to indulge in the "build it yourself,
       add, test, submit patches" paradigm of
       NetHack-now.

    i) whatever is chosen should in _its_ design have
       targeted extreme hardware/OS portability, so
       that the next NetHack, in using it, can do the
       same

I'd like to propose a second and third level of
issues, both based on the necessity to last for
decades:

1) how do you fund it?

    a) where does the code sit (Subversion tree, etc.)
       and who pays for that?

    b) how does the ongoing current product get
       distributed?

    c) how do you survive the advance of technology,
       or, if economies and energy supplies both
       continue downhill, the retreat of technology?

    d) will there be server play? Who funds the
       server time, who manages the server(s)?

    e) whatever is chosen has to be something expected
       to be stable for decades; I wouldn't trust any
       company as a funding agent to last for decades
       in the current economic increasing churn
       state.

    f) can we fund the effort with advertising? Would
       we want to?

    g) notice that Rogue, parent of the Rogue-likes,
       was developed as a commercial game; would it
       work to put a small price on the next NetHack?

2) how do you organize its creation?

    a) the development of NetHack, Python, Linux, ...
       were based on a "strong leader" organization,
       with one person making final decisions, but
       loss of interest is a real problem, so is
       mortality

    b) other open source projects, like Mozilla, seem
       to be nearly anarchies [but what's going on
       behind the scenes?]

    c) who hands out the work, who architects the
       program, who designs the parts, who does the
       coding, who does the pre-alpha SQA testing,
       who writes the test scripts, ...

    d) does development go in a conventional way, or
       in a code-to-test model, or in a rapid
       development model, or what?

I'm sure I've touched 1% of each issue, I'd just
like to see more thought given to them early on.

